              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The \"Adam\u2019s apple\"\u2014physically known as the **laryngeal prominence**\u2014is a bump formed by the **thyroid cartilage**, a shield-like structure that wraps around and protects your larynx (voice box) and vocal cords. \n\nWhile both men and women have thyroid cartilage, men typically have much larger and more visible Adam\u2019s apples. This difference comes down to three main factors: **hormones, the size of the voice box, and the angle of the cartilage.**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Role of Testosterone during Puberty\nBefore puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized voice boxes and thyroid cartilages. However, during puberty, biological males experience a massive surge in testosterone. \n\nThis hormone triggers the rapid growth of the larynx, causing the vocal cords to grow thicker and longer (which is what deepens a man's voice). Because the larynx grows so much larger, the protective thyroid cartilage surrounding it must also expand to accommodate the larger structure.\n\n### 2. The Angle of the Cartilage (The Key Difference)\nEven when the cartilage grows, its visibility is mostly determined by *how* the cartilage plates join together. \n\nThe thyroid cartilage is made of two flat plates (called laminae) that meet at the front of the throat, forming a V-shape. The angle at which these two plates fuse differs dramatically between sexes:\n* **In males:** The plates fuse at a much sharper, more acute angle\u2014typically around **90 degrees**. This sharp, narrow V-shape juts forward sharply, creating a prominent protrusion under the skin.\n* **In females:** The plates fuse at a much wider, gentler angle\u2014typically around **120 degrees**. This wider curve lies relatively flat against the neck, making the cartilage much less noticeable, even though it is still there.\n\n### 3. Voice Resonance\nThe difference in size acts like a musical instrument. Just as a double bass produces a deeper sound than a violin because it has a larger, more spacious hollow body, a larger larynx and thyroid cartilage create a larger resonance chamber. This allows men to produce deeper, lower-pitched vocal tones. \n\n### Summary\nMen have larger Adam\u2019s apples because testosterone causes their voice boxes to grow larger during puberty. To protect this larger voice box, the surrounding cartilage fuses at a sharp **90-degree angle**, causing it to protrude forward, whereas women's cartilage fuses at a flatter **120-degree angle** and remains relatively hidden.",
